MECHANISMS OF DISEASE: the four categories of the Gene Curation Coalition (GenCC). Each is judged on its own for one disease of the gene with one mode of inheritance, and more than one can hold for the same disease.

- loss_of_function (LOF): the disease-causing variants reduce or abolish the normal function of the gene or its product. Monoallelic (haploinsufficiency) or biallelic.
- gain_of_function (GOF): the variants enhance the product's function or alter it in a way that drives the disease: increased or constitutive activity, loss of regulation, expression in a new tissue or at a new time, or a new function.
- dominant_negative (DN): the altered product is expressed and interferes with the product of the normal allele, so that activity falls below what the loss of one allele would leave. Only a monoallelic disease can have it.
- not_loss_of_function (NOT LOF): the evidence shows that loss of function is not the mechanism of this disease. It can hold while the true mechanism is still unclear, and next to GOF or DN.

EVIDENCE THAT COUNTS (GenCC's mechanism curation recommendations, version 1.0):

- For LOF: predicted loss-of-function variants (pLOF: nonsense, frameshift, canonical ±1/±2 splice site, start loss, deletion of one or more exons) among the variants that cause the disease, expected to undergo nonsense-mediated decay; other variants with variant-level functional evidence of an absent or degraded transcript or protein; a knock-out model organism with a consistent phenotype and zygosity; population data consistent with LOF (constraint against pLOF variants for a monoallelic disease, no or very few homozygous pLOF variants in controls for a biallelic disease).
- For GOF: strong variant-level functional evidence of a gain of function; a knock-in or transgenic model organism that demonstrates it, with a consistent phenotype and zygosity; a known function of the gene that supports it; studies consistent with a gain of function (new expression, mislocalisation, accumulation, a new function); a knock-out model with no phenotype or a different one; no pLOF variants among the causal variants, or pLOF variants that cause a different disease; more than 3 recurrent variants in a cluster.
- For DN: strong variant-level functional evidence of a dominant-negative effect, with the altered allele expressed; a knock-in or transgenic model organism that demonstrates it; a known function of the gene that supports it, such as a product that forms a multimer; a highly homologous protein with a well-established dominant-negative mechanism; patients or model organisms with knock-out variants whose phenotype is directionally concordant; more than 3 recurrent variants in a cluster.
- For NOT LOF: pLOF variants or deletions of the gene in unaffected individuals with the zygosity in question, or in individuals with a different phenotype; a knock-out model organism without the phenotype; functional experiments that show that loss of function is not the mechanism; pLOF variants that are frequent in population databases, or no constraint against them, given the severity, onset and inheritance of the disease.
